00:02:42.585 --> 00:02:44.710
And so we're going to talk
about some of the things

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:47.620
that you can take advantage
of on Compute Engine

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:48.760
to address that concern.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:54.010
So before I go any
further, usually this slide

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:56.350
comes at the end, but this
is an important enough topic.

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:57.850
Obviously, we've
put a lot of effort

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:00.690
into making sure that we have
a lot of great content for you

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:02.830
here at Cloud Next
over the coming days.

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:05.320
And so I wanted to call
out some of the other great

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:07.780
presentations that you can
either see here or catch

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:10.916
on YouTube that go into some
of the more detail on some

00:03:10.916 --> 00:03:14.050
of the tools and techniques that
are outside Compute Engine's

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:17.740
features itself but within
our billing support systems

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:21.490
that you can use to help
you with this problem.

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:23.140
And I'll give you
a little preview

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:24.306
of some of those at the end.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:33.360
So since food is probably on
the minds of a lot of people

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:35.490
here, or maybe not
because you just ate,

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:37.620
I thought I would keep
this a little bit light.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:39.720
I have a little bit of a
metaphor running through

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:41.940
as if this were a cooking show.

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:44.490
And I'm going to teach you how
to cook up an efficient Cloud

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:45.150
bill.

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:47.020
So bear with me.

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:49.554
I won't overstretch the
metaphor too much hopefully.

00:03:49.554 --> 00:03:51.720
But I'm going to start with
sharing some basic ideas

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:53.970
about cooking your
bill, and then

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:55.650
move on to some
of the ingredients

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:58.789
that you can play with
to get the best outcome.

00:03:58.789 --> 00:04:00.330
And then I'll talk
about some recipes

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:01.496
that you should try at home.

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:07.240
And then we have some
gadgets at the end.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:08.584
So chopping onions.

00:04:08.584 --> 00:04:10.750
That's kind of where most
people start with cooking.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:11.958
That's a good thing to learn.

00:04:11.958 --> 00:04:14.714
So what are some of the basic
ideas about cost management

00:04:14.714 --> 00:04:16.089
that you should
be thinking about

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:19.910
as you're looking about
how you use Compute Engine?

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:23.264
Well, so first of all, we're
working on pricing innovations.

00:04:23.264 --> 00:04:25.430
And when we're thinking
about what do we want to do,

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:28.550
we keep three core
principles in mind.

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:30.380
And those are the
ones you see here.

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:33.050
It should be simple,
it should be flexible,

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:34.670
and it should be fair.

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:36.529
So those are big
words and they can

00:04:36.529 --> 00:04:37.820
mean a lot of different things.

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:41.090
I'm going to tell you what they
mean to me and to our team when

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:42.650
we're working on these things.

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:45.110
And we think that these
are the ideals that

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:47.919
represent what customers tell
us they would like to have.

00:04:47.919 --> 00:04:49.710
And so we really try
to keep those in mind.

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:54.360
So that guy was pretty smart.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:55.860
He had a good idea.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:58.350
Simple is nice to say,
but obviously, Cloud

00:04:58.350 --> 00:04:59.760
is a very complicated space.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:02.760
So what does it mean to have a
very simple approach to pricing

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:05.430
in a complex space?

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:09.060
It doesn't mean everything
is exactly the same price.

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:11.970
It doesn't mean
there's not any dials

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:13.530
or knobs you can optimize.

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:16.140
What it should mean is that
the concepts are simple,

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:18.010
that it's easy for
you, as a customer,

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:22.200
to understand how it works,
how changes would happen

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:24.630
if you make changes
to your usage,

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:26.620
and then it all
kind of makes sense.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:29.910
So you don't have to have a PhD
in economics, a PhD in physics,

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:32.940
or a team of people
figuring out how this works.

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:34.540
You can kind of hold
it in your head.

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:38.610
That's the idea of simple
that we're going for.

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:41.810
So like I said, simple to
understand, simple to use,

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:44.150
but powerful enough
to meet your needs.

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:46.940
And you have a lot of complex
needs as a set of customers.

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:48.567
There's a lot of
different use cases.

00:05:48.567 --> 00:05:50.900
And so we want to be able to
capture those and make sure

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:54.650
that what you're getting
works for your situation,

00:05:54.650 --> 00:05:57.140
but no rocket science, and
definitely not hidden costs.

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:02.230
So then what does flexible mean?

00:06:02.230 --> 00:06:04.390
Well, like I said,
across our customer

00:06:04.390 --> 00:06:07.300
base there is a very broad
set of different use cases.

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:08.724
Not all businesses are alike.

00:06:08.724 --> 00:06:11.140
Not all teams are thinking
about spending in the same way.

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:13.000
That might be
because the finance

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:16.450
department or the IT
division or whoever

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:18.617
thinks about how
this money is spent

00:06:18.617 --> 00:06:20.950
might have a different approach
to how the controls work

00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:22.824
within that company.

00:06:22.824 --> 00:06:24.240
It might be that
you're a startup,

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:27.540
and the finance department
is Joe or Sally,

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:29.610
and it's their credit card.

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:31.590
Or it might be a much
more complex situation.

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:34.173
We have another talk, actually,
that talks about how you could

00:06:34.173 --> 00:06:35.760
scale that whole challenge.

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:37.930
Also, your needs are
going to change over time.

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:40.950
So something that you
bought 18 months ago

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:43.664
or signed up for three years
on, if it's not flexible enough,

00:06:43.664 --> 00:06:46.080
it's not going to meet your
needs by the end of your time.

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:47.979
So we think a lot about that.

00:06:47.979 --> 00:06:50.020
And what that really boils
down to in our product

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:51.770
is that you should be
able to pay for what

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:53.352
you need, not anything more.

00:06:53.352 --> 00:06:55.810
And you shouldn't have regrets
when those things do change,

00:06:55.810 --> 00:06:57.530
or feel like you
can't make changes,

00:06:57.530 --> 00:07:02.360
because then you wouldn't be
efficient with your spend.

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:04.340
And finally, fair.

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:05.870
So I have a bunch--

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:07.510
I have three small kids.

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:10.410
And my five-year-old-- I'm
sure you know the phrase,

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:12.950
but that's not fair.

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:16.160
So we're working a lot on
what the word fair means.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:18.290
It doesn't mean what
he thinks it means.

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:21.920
What it means in
this context is what

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:23.960
works for Google works for you.

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:28.690
So Moore's law happens, prices
go down for our components,

00:07:28.690 --> 00:07:29.757
performance goes up.

00:07:29.757 --> 00:07:31.340
You should see that
reflected, just as

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:33.320
if you were buying
the machines yourself.

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:35.130
You should always be
getting a great price.

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:37.620
And you should always be
getting a great value.

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:40.730
So a lot of times we talk about
price performance, not just

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:42.926
price.

00:07:42.926 --> 00:07:45.550
That also has led to some of the
innovative features that we've

00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:48.490
brought out, such as
automatic discounts.

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:51.310
You don't have to ask us or
learn the secret trick to know

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:52.320
how to use the discount.

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:54.070
It just happens.

00:07:54.070 --> 00:07:56.429
We think that's a pretty
cool innovation that is fair.

00:07:56.429 --> 00:07:57.970
If it's more efficient
for us to run,

00:07:57.970 --> 00:08:00.660
we should give you some of
that back as a discount.

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:02.260
It also has led to
us building tools

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:04.000
like rightsizing
recommendations, which

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:06.770
is if you're overspending,
we will actually say,

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:08.710
hey, you're overspending.

00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:10.200
You should stop doing that.

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:13.490
That's a little counterintuitive
for a for-profit company,

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:13.990
right?

00:08:13.990 --> 00:08:15.850
We're telling you to
spend less with us.

00:08:15.850 --> 00:08:18.820
But we believe that that is
the fair and right thing to do,

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:22.150
and that either you will
use that somewhere else

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:23.980
for something that
is valuable to you

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:26.080
or we will sell it
to another customer.

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:28.479
And we're happy either way.

00:08:28.479 --> 00:08:30.020
And then finally,
we want to give you

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:31.790
smart tools and the
data that you need to be

00:08:31.790 --> 00:08:33.081
able to monitor and control it.

00:08:33.081 --> 00:08:35.497
So there's no questions
about what you're spending.

00:08:35.497 --> 00:08:36.830
That's what we think fair means.

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:39.830
OK.

00:08:39.830 --> 00:08:41.152
So some terminology.

00:08:41.152 --> 00:08:42.860
Different people are
at different places.

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:44.210
But I want to
highlight a few things

00:08:44.210 --> 00:08:46.130
that we have customer
conversations with quite

00:08:46.130 --> 00:08:46.970
frequently.

00:08:46.970 --> 00:08:49.760
And the first one
is what's a core?

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:51.602
What's a vCPU?

00:08:51.602 --> 00:08:52.560
What's a physical core?

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:53.660
What's a virtual core?

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:59.510
So on the left is a somewhat
older generation x86 CPU.

00:08:59.510 --> 00:09:04.910
And if you squint
you can see here

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:07.670
there are four what I
call physical cores,

00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:10.830
actual execution units that have
all of their stuff duplicated.

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:12.990
It's actually a mirror image.

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:14.597
And there's four
of those on there.

00:09:14.597 --> 00:09:16.430
So a lot of times a
customer will come to us

00:09:16.430 --> 00:09:18.200
and say, well, I have
a four core machine.

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:21.050
If you bought this processor
it would be called a quad core

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:22.980
Intel CPU.

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:25.216
But there's a technology
called hyper-threading,

00:09:25.216 --> 00:09:27.090
which I'm sure many of
you are familiar with,

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:28.923
which essentially allows
each of those cores

00:09:28.923 --> 00:09:32.470
to run two different paths of
execution at the same time.

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:35.210
So in Cloud, those
are commonly exposed

00:09:35.210 --> 00:09:38.040
of as what we'd call a
virtual core independently,

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:39.370
or a vcore, or a vCPU.

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:44.760
So it's kind of little
something like that.

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:48.027
So that's a four core processor.

00:09:48.027 --> 00:09:50.110
If we were selling that
as a VM, which we wouldn't

00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:53.085
do because that's not enough
cores to put in a server,

00:09:53.085 --> 00:09:54.820
but if we were
selling that, that

00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:57.070
would be an eight vCPU machine.

00:09:57.070 --> 00:10:01.150
And we would sell an
eight vCPU VM on it.

00:10:01.150 --> 00:10:05.740
Seems obvious, perhaps,
but the difference is 2x.

00:10:05.740 --> 00:10:08.110
So if you're analyzing
your costs incorrectly

00:10:08.110 --> 00:10:10.720
thinking about what you
need, the difference

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:11.590
can be significant.

00:10:11.590 --> 00:10:14.740
And so when we're having
conversations with customers

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:17.440
about what size
their VM should be

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:19.810
and how that scales, in
particular if they're migrating

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:22.032
something from an
on-prem environment,

00:10:22.032 --> 00:10:23.740
this is a topic that
comes up very often.

00:10:23.740 --> 00:10:26.760
So I wanted to
highlight it here.

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:29.470
So count those cores carefully.

00:10:29.470 --> 00:10:32.400
But the thing is,
our hardware might

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:36.510
be newer than what you have, the
performance might be different.

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:38.700
Your software may
or may not be clock

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:40.950
rated or thread sensitive.

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:44.310
So it's not always quite
as straightforward as 2x,

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:46.259
and we'll talk a little
bit later about why

00:10:46.259 --> 00:10:47.550
it's important to measure that.

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:49.350
But it is important
to look at this.

00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:50.850
Now, the other thing
to keep in mind

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:52.920
is that you may have
software licenses.

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:55.540
For example, Microsoft licenses
either for Windows Server

00:10:55.540 --> 00:10:57.540
or for SQL Server
tend to measure

00:10:57.540 --> 00:10:59.310
in terms of physical cores.

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:00.880
Sometimes when
you move to Cloud,

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:02.400
they decide to use
that same license

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:03.810
to apply to a virtual core.

00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:05.170
And that can change your costs.

00:11:05.170 --> 00:11:06.794
So that's another
thing to be aware of.

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:11.870
Also, I want to talk about
analyzing your needs.

00:11:11.870 --> 00:11:15.420
So looking across
clouds, for example,

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:18.020
list prices are not
exactly the same.

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:20.000
The machines are not
necessarily the same.

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:22.820
The way that you pay is
not necessarily the same.

00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:24.950
You're not actually
likely to pay list price

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:27.920
unless you're doing a very
unusual usage pattern.

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:29.729
But the list prices
still are not the same.

00:11:29.729 --> 00:11:31.520
So if that's where you
start your analysis,

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:33.470
you're already not
quite looking at it

00:11:33.470 --> 00:11:35.630
the way it's going to
show up on your bill.

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:38.240
You need to understand how
the different clouds discount

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:41.010
differently and what your usage
pattern is going to look like.

00:11:41.010 --> 00:11:43.760
So this kind of leads to
a similar tip, which is

00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:45.230
think about your analysis.

00:11:45.230 --> 00:11:48.500
Try to come as close as you can
to modeling what your usage is

00:11:48.500 --> 00:11:49.670
really going to look like.

00:11:49.670 --> 00:11:51.500
A lot of times a proof
of concept use case

00:11:51.500 --> 00:11:54.650
can help with this.

00:11:54.650 --> 00:11:57.580
So finally, that broad idea of
understanding what your usage

00:11:57.580 --> 00:11:58.370
is.

00:11:58.370 --> 00:12:00.070
What are the underlying trends?

00:12:00.070 --> 00:12:04.120
How peaky or spikey is
your workload going to be?

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:06.106
And then once you
understand that,

00:12:06.106 --> 00:12:07.480
it's important to
also understand

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:10.290
that the true utilization-- what
I'll call true utilization--

00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:13.270
is not actually how many
cores you've deployed.

00:12:13.270 --> 00:12:16.600
If they're all idle, you're not
getting any value out of those.

00:12:16.600 --> 00:12:19.165
And so the ROI on those
you can call zero.

00:12:19.165 --> 00:12:21.040
You're paying for
something you're not using.

00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:23.620
I would say it's actually
negative, because it's still

00:12:23.620 --> 00:12:24.510
burning power.

00:12:24.510 --> 00:12:26.320
And so when you're
thinking about comparing

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:28.903
one situation to another, it's
important to keep that in mind.

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:35.490
Finally, TCO-- that's not
usually what it stands for,

00:12:35.490 --> 00:12:37.290
but it's a very
confusing topic so that's

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:39.481
what I'm going to call it today.

00:12:39.481 --> 00:12:40.980
So customers
oftentimes when they're

00:12:40.980 --> 00:12:42.830
calculating their
internal TCO, this

00:12:42.830 --> 00:12:45.330
turns into a big conversation
about what have they included,

00:12:45.330 --> 00:12:46.572
what have they not included.

00:12:46.572 --> 00:12:48.030
The point that I
want to make here,

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:49.830
though, is that
even once you get

00:12:49.830 --> 00:12:53.340
that whole financial analysis
you're kind of not done.

00:12:53.340 --> 00:12:57.570
Because your on-prem
configuration is a much longer

00:12:57.570 --> 00:12:58.950
time frame environment.

00:12:58.950 --> 00:13:01.740
So from your finance
department, you'll

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:05.694
hear, well, our TCO is x dollars
or x cents per core hour.

00:13:05.694 --> 00:13:07.110
And that's on a
machine that maybe

00:13:07.110 --> 00:13:09.990
has a three year or a five year
depreciation schedule depending

00:13:09.990 --> 00:13:11.130
on your refresh.

00:13:11.130 --> 00:13:11.940
But guess what?

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:14.410
That hardware's out
of date in 18 months.

00:13:14.410 --> 00:13:17.280
So you have it for another year
and a half to 3 and 1/2 years.

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:18.750
And that's what you get on it.

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:20.802
You're not getting
performance upgrades.

00:13:20.802 --> 00:13:22.260
You may not have
other accelerators

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:23.530
that you can plug into it.

00:13:23.530 --> 00:13:26.150
You may not be able to upgrade
your racks to have more power.

00:13:26.150 --> 00:13:28.544
You may not be able to
change your networking gear.

00:13:28.544 --> 00:13:30.210
You may not be able
to resize your disks

00:13:30.210 --> 00:13:32.070
by making a single click.

00:13:32.070 --> 00:13:34.740
So there's a lot of other
limitations and flexibility

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:37.320
you lose in that calculation.

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:40.110
And that doesn't always show
up in the actual dollars

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:42.984
per watt kind of calculation.

00:13:42.984 --> 00:13:45.150
So it's important consideration
to keep in mind when

00:13:45.150 --> 00:13:47.190
you're thinking about
whether the prices are

00:13:47.190 --> 00:13:50.440
apples to apples.

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:54.040
OK, so let's talk about
some of the ingredients that

00:13:54.040 --> 00:13:56.640
go into our cost saving
souffle, if you will.

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:02.100
First of all, the way we
think about it at Google

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:04.320
is there's a set
of base resources.

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:06.210
Pretty much
fundamentally, a computer

00:14:06.210 --> 00:14:09.870
is a processing unit
and some RAM for data.

00:14:09.870 --> 00:14:12.570
And then there's some I/O that
brings the data in and out.

00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:16.140
So in a lot of places
where you might deploy,

00:14:16.140 --> 00:14:20.370
that ratio of how many vCPUs
to how much gigabytes of RAM

00:14:20.370 --> 00:14:25.370
is fixed, or at least it's fixed
among a small set of options.

00:14:25.370 --> 00:14:28.510
The problem with that is it
might not match your workload.

00:14:28.510 --> 00:14:30.600
So that's one aspect.

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:32.170
The second aspect
is what we would

00:14:32.170 --> 00:14:34.480
think of as the attached
resources, things

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:36.830
that you could imagine you're
plugging into a server.

00:14:36.830 --> 00:14:38.747
In reality, they
may already be there

00:14:38.747 --> 00:14:41.080
or your VM is just going to
be assigned to a server that

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:42.380
happens to have those things.

00:14:42.380 --> 00:14:44.588
And then you're going to
use them when you need them.

00:14:44.588 --> 00:14:46.180
But how do you
think about those?

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:48.220
Are those essentially
welded into the VM

00:14:48.220 --> 00:14:49.330
that you need to buy?

00:14:49.330 --> 00:14:51.220
Or do you get to
choose dynamically

00:14:51.220 --> 00:14:57.680
or in a very granular way what
you need and then pay for that?

00:14:57.680 --> 00:15:02.030
So looking at that and thinking
about the problem that way

00:15:02.030 --> 00:15:04.970
and thinking about having
a decomposed solution

00:15:04.970 --> 00:15:09.170
led us to looking at this idea
of alphabet letters and shapes

00:15:09.170 --> 00:15:13.170
and sizes and Z1 and X7
and all of this stuff.

00:15:13.170 --> 00:15:16.824
And the fact that those have
these pre-configured shapes

00:15:16.824 --> 00:15:17.990
of what you're going to get.

00:15:17.990 --> 00:15:20.600
You're going to get a
certain vCPU in RAM ratio.

00:15:20.600 --> 00:15:22.920
You're going to get
certain devices plugged in.

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:26.690
And the providers
that do it that way

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:29.690
are trying to guess at what
your use case is going to be.

00:15:29.690 --> 00:15:33.092
But they're doing one guess
for a broad set of customers.

00:15:33.092 --> 00:15:35.550
And even within those use cases,
things might be different.

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:38.266
So if you want to use
a bunch of NVIDIA GPUs,

00:15:38.266 --> 00:15:39.890
you might have very
different use cases

00:15:39.890 --> 00:15:42.431
and very different patterns that
mean you need more CPU, less

00:15:42.431 --> 00:15:44.080
CPU, more RAM, less RAM.

00:15:44.080 --> 00:15:47.835
So when you're
thinking about cost,

00:15:47.835 --> 00:15:49.960
you need to think about
the fact that it's not just

00:15:49.960 --> 00:15:52.930
the dollars per hour
cost of that VM,

00:15:52.930 --> 00:15:55.570
that pre-configured shape.

00:15:55.570 --> 00:15:58.270
If you're not using
some of those resources,

00:15:58.270 --> 00:16:01.090
you're just paying for
waste that you don't need.

00:16:01.090 --> 00:16:03.950
Now, it turns out that
getting this right

00:16:03.950 --> 00:16:07.100
and doing this in a decomposed
way is a really tricky problem.

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:10.310
Fortunately, Google
internally uses

00:16:10.310 --> 00:16:12.279
a system called Borg
to manage our fleet.

00:16:12.279 --> 00:16:13.820
And you've probably
read a little bit

00:16:13.820 --> 00:16:15.440
about that in the
press, maybe not.

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:19.280
But that's how we managed
our fleet for over a decade.

00:16:19.280 --> 00:16:21.500
And it's gotten
very good at having

00:16:21.500 --> 00:16:23.780
flexible definition
of what a job uses

00:16:23.780 --> 00:16:25.430
and how many resources it needs.

00:16:25.430 --> 00:16:28.880
And it understands how to
pack those things together

00:16:28.880 --> 00:16:30.200
in an efficient way.

00:16:30.200 --> 00:16:33.740
And Compute Engine is built on
top of that core technology.

00:16:33.740 --> 00:16:35.360
And it uses and
leverages all of that.

00:16:35.360 --> 00:16:39.809
So for us, having more flexible
definition of what a VM is

00:16:39.809 --> 00:16:41.600
was an easy thing to
do, because we already

00:16:41.600 --> 00:16:43.190
had that infrastructure there.

00:16:43.190 --> 00:16:45.200
If you don't have that
infrastructure, making

00:16:45.200 --> 00:16:48.830
everything scale in a
fixed size and ratcheting

00:16:48.830 --> 00:16:52.040
it up by powers of two makes for
a relatively simple scheduling

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:52.820
problem.

00:16:52.820 --> 00:16:55.200
And you can efficiently
schedule the machine.

00:16:55.200 --> 00:16:58.760
The problem is any waste is
the customer's responsibility

00:16:58.760 --> 00:16:59.280
to pay for.

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:01.738
And that's not what we wanted
to end up with in our design.

00:17:04.894 --> 00:17:06.310
OK, so what are
some of the things

00:17:06.310 --> 00:17:08.230
you can do with
these ingredients,

00:17:08.230 --> 00:17:11.470
as it were, on Compute Engine
to cook up some savings?

00:17:14.319 --> 00:17:16.214
So the direction
I've been leading

00:17:16.214 --> 00:17:17.589
with the last
couple of slides is

00:17:17.589 --> 00:17:21.170
talking about where we landed
with custom machine types.

00:17:21.170 --> 00:17:23.470
So if you haven't seen
this feature before,

00:17:23.470 --> 00:17:29.210
essentially the idea is
pick the ratio you want,

00:17:29.210 --> 00:17:32.740
decide what you want
attached, and you're done.

00:17:32.740 --> 00:17:34.350
That's what you pay for.

00:17:34.350 --> 00:17:39.390
So we allow for any increments
of either from one vCPU

00:17:39.390 --> 00:17:42.570
and then any multiple of two
up to the largest machine

00:17:42.570 --> 00:17:43.950
size that we offer.

00:17:43.950 --> 00:17:48.690
And RAM within a very
broad ratio of about 0.9

00:17:48.690 --> 00:17:51.600
gigabytes to 6.5
gigabytes per CPU.

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:55.020
So it's a ratio range that you
can tune in quarter gigabyte

00:17:55.020 --> 00:17:55.920
increments.

00:17:55.920 --> 00:17:58.050
So what that means
is pretty much

00:17:58.050 --> 00:18:02.910
any ratio you've got out there,
unless it's 14 to 1, 16 to 1

00:18:02.910 --> 00:18:07.890
on RAM, you can build your
own custom machine type that

00:18:07.890 --> 00:18:09.420
matches that ratio exactly.

00:18:09.420 --> 00:18:11.520
Then you can pick
how much storage

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:12.750
you want to attach to it.

00:18:12.750 --> 00:18:14.790
You can pick if you
want local SSD attached

00:18:14.790 --> 00:18:18.330
to it, GPUs, Cloud TPUs.

00:18:18.330 --> 00:18:20.670
You configure that and then
that's what you pay for.

00:18:20.670 --> 00:18:24.290
And we show you the prices
of those individual units.

00:18:24.290 --> 00:18:26.030
So one of the
things that we found

00:18:26.030 --> 00:18:28.070
is that this was very
helpful for customers

00:18:28.070 --> 00:18:29.990
that are doing
migrations, particularly

00:18:29.990 --> 00:18:34.820
from on-prem, where in the past,
processor manufacturers have

00:18:34.820 --> 00:18:38.300
made machines that are six
cores or processors that

00:18:38.300 --> 00:18:42.410
are six cores, 12, 14, 18.

00:18:42.410 --> 00:18:44.280
Those aren't powers of 2.

00:18:44.280 --> 00:18:47.300
So if you're actually looking to
migrate an existing application

00:18:47.300 --> 00:18:50.510
without many changes, the thing
that might make the most sense

00:18:50.510 --> 00:18:53.420
is to be able to size it into
a VM that exactly matches

00:18:53.420 --> 00:18:55.200
the same number of resources.

00:18:55.200 --> 00:18:56.726
And so this lets you do that.

00:18:56.726 --> 00:18:58.100
Now, we'll talk
in a minute about

00:18:58.100 --> 00:18:59.808
whether or not that's
as far as you could

00:18:59.808 --> 00:19:01.190
go in optimizing your spend.

00:19:01.190 --> 00:19:03.960
But it's a reasonable starting
point and it helps you already.

00:19:03.960 --> 00:19:05.540
So if you thought
about what you'd

00:19:05.540 --> 00:19:09.770
do migrating to a cloud that
didn't have that option, if you

00:19:09.770 --> 00:19:16.970
need 10 vCPUs, and your only
option is one that has 16,

00:19:16.970 --> 00:19:20.211
you're already 60% over
on processing power.

00:19:20.211 --> 00:19:20.960
So what do you do?

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:22.530
Do you squeeze it down to eight?

00:19:22.530 --> 00:19:26.640
Do you run 16 and hope it's OK
and just take the extra costs?

00:19:26.640 --> 00:19:29.970
You don't have to make
that choice on Google.

00:19:29.970 --> 00:19:32.330
Another thing that we
see is for some reason--

00:19:32.330 --> 00:19:35.540
it's pretty strange,
we're not really sure--

00:19:35.540 --> 00:19:38.959
a lot of people run
2 gigabytes per vCPU.

00:19:38.959 --> 00:19:40.250
We do actually know the reason.

00:19:40.250 --> 00:19:43.920
It's because that's a ratio
that exists on another cloud.

00:19:43.920 --> 00:19:46.560
So it's very easy to port over
from that perspective as well.

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:48.851
It's not a ratio that we
offer in our predefined types,

00:19:48.851 --> 00:19:50.359
but it is a common ratio.

00:19:50.359 --> 00:19:52.025
So it can help with
that too if you just

00:19:52.025 --> 00:19:54.090
want to minimize your changes.

00:19:54.090 --> 00:19:57.740
The nice thing about this is you
can also just optimize later.

00:19:57.740 --> 00:19:59.790
You can start where
it makes sense,

00:19:59.790 --> 00:20:02.270
and then if you want to go
spend some time maybe doing

00:20:02.270 --> 00:20:04.100
some deep analysis
of your application

00:20:04.100 --> 00:20:07.340
later, figure out exactly
how much RAM you really need,

00:20:07.340 --> 00:20:10.700
just move the dial,
turn down the RAM.

00:20:10.700 --> 00:20:13.160
Or maybe you're finding
that your server is sitting

00:20:13.160 --> 00:20:16.490
idle, you need fewer
processors, move the dial,

00:20:16.490 --> 00:20:18.020
turn down the processors.

00:20:18.020 --> 00:20:20.732
And then a quick reboot
and you're good to go.

00:20:20.732 --> 00:20:22.190
We'd like to get
rid of the reboot,

00:20:22.190 --> 00:20:24.720
but it turns on a lot of guest
operating systems require it.

00:20:24.720 --> 00:20:28.360
So maybe some day.

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:31.300
But wait, there's more.

00:20:31.300 --> 00:20:33.050
We'll actually
help you with this.

00:20:33.050 --> 00:20:36.009
So last year, we talked about
right sizing recommendations

00:20:36.009 --> 00:20:36.550
a little bit.

00:20:36.550 --> 00:20:38.920
At that time, the
feature was in beta.

00:20:38.920 --> 00:20:41.060
It's now generally available.

00:20:41.060 --> 00:20:44.170
And this was actually a snapshot
of part of the Cloud console.

00:20:44.170 --> 00:20:47.070
And so the feature is
the right-hand column.

00:20:47.070 --> 00:20:50.679
Essentially, we will watch
your VM from the outside,

00:20:50.679 --> 00:20:51.970
without seeing any of the data.

00:20:51.970 --> 00:20:55.960
We look at the usage of
CPU utilization, RAM,

00:20:55.960 --> 00:20:57.500
page utilization.

00:20:57.500 --> 00:21:00.550
And we can figure out how much
you are leaving idle and not

00:21:00.550 --> 00:21:01.510
using.

00:21:01.510 --> 00:21:03.230
And after we've watched
that for a while,

00:21:03.230 --> 00:21:06.650
we will make a recommendation
to you about a different shape

00:21:06.650 --> 00:21:07.420
you could pick.

00:21:07.420 --> 00:21:09.700
Oftentimes, it's going
to be a custom shape that

00:21:09.700 --> 00:21:11.360
will allow you to save money.

00:21:11.360 --> 00:21:14.230
We evaluate the RAM and
CPU dimensions separately.

00:21:14.230 --> 00:21:17.080
So essentially, we're
moving the knob for you

00:21:17.080 --> 00:21:19.920
on custom machine types.

00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:22.720
And then it's with one-click
you can apply that.

00:21:22.720 --> 00:21:25.920
In addition, we currently have
in beta this same capability,

00:21:25.920 --> 00:21:29.400
but for Managed Instance
Groups that are not autoscaled.

00:21:29.400 --> 00:21:32.351
So autoscaling is a
little bit harder problem,

00:21:32.351 --> 00:21:34.350
because you're essentially
scaling horizontally.

00:21:34.350 --> 00:21:38.310
So what does it mean to resize
any individual instance is

00:21:38.310 --> 00:21:39.600
a trickier thing.

00:21:39.600 --> 00:21:41.880
But we see people use
Managed Instance Groups

00:21:41.880 --> 00:21:45.270
for scenarios like a cluster of
database machines, for example.

00:21:45.270 --> 00:21:48.040
It's not one that
changes in size.

00:21:48.040 --> 00:21:50.520
But it might be that each
of those individual machines

00:21:50.520 --> 00:21:53.250
are oversized in one
or both dimensions.

00:21:53.250 --> 00:21:55.590
So that capability
is also there.

00:21:55.590 --> 00:21:58.110
Now, one caveat I'll
mention and the other reason

00:21:58.110 --> 00:22:00.780
why we do have the button for
you to click and confirm it

00:22:00.780 --> 00:22:03.690
is there are outlier cases
where you might actually

00:22:03.690 --> 00:22:06.390
intentionally want that
machine to be over-provisioned.

00:22:06.390 --> 00:22:08.100
And an example I
would give you is

00:22:08.100 --> 00:22:10.510
if you have a certain
latency target on, say,

00:22:10.510 --> 00:22:12.930
a query response
from a server, you

00:22:12.930 --> 00:22:16.889
might need the processor
to be not fully utilized so

00:22:16.889 --> 00:22:18.180
that you can hit that reliably.

00:22:18.180 --> 00:22:20.719
Because when it starts to
get highly utilized, then

00:22:20.719 --> 00:22:22.510
you're going to get
more variance at you're

00:22:22.510 --> 00:22:23.850
P90 or your P99.

00:22:23.850 --> 00:22:26.460
So there are some cases where
that doesn't necessarily

00:22:26.460 --> 00:22:30.130
make sense, but for
most people this really

00:22:30.130 --> 00:22:33.012
helps you move the dial
on what your spend is

00:22:33.012 --> 00:22:33.970
and how much is wasted.

00:22:38.144 --> 00:22:40.310
So another thing that we
do a little bit differently

00:22:40.310 --> 00:22:43.620
on Google Cloud to help
you save automatically

00:22:43.620 --> 00:22:45.290
is Sustained Use Discounts.

00:22:45.290 --> 00:22:48.270
So the core idea
here is very simple.

00:22:48.270 --> 00:22:52.200
The more you run the
more you save, up to 30%

00:22:52.200 --> 00:22:54.410
if you run a VM for
the whole month.

00:22:54.410 --> 00:22:57.300
Now, it turns out that's
fairly straightforward,

00:22:57.300 --> 00:22:59.150
but that use case of
you have to run it

00:22:59.150 --> 00:23:01.490
for exactly the whole
month might not always

00:23:01.490 --> 00:23:03.170
happen in production scenarios.

00:23:03.170 --> 00:23:05.420
Like, let's say you need
to reboot or upgrade

00:23:05.420 --> 00:23:09.460
or you move to a different
zone or instance type.

00:23:09.460 --> 00:23:11.180
There are some of
those scenarios where

00:23:11.180 --> 00:23:13.850
it wouldn't be fair, if you
refer to the principles,

00:23:13.850 --> 00:23:14.910
for us to say, yep.

00:23:14.910 --> 00:23:15.410
Nope, sorry.

00:23:15.410 --> 00:23:17.285
You don't get the discount
because you didn't

00:23:17.285 --> 00:23:19.320
meet these type of criteria.

00:23:19.320 --> 00:23:22.430
The actual concept here is
if your usage is steady,

00:23:22.430 --> 00:23:25.160
it's more efficient for us to
provide the service for you.

00:23:25.160 --> 00:23:28.680
And so in that case, we're very
happy to give you a discount.

00:23:28.680 --> 00:23:33.170
So we came up with a concept
called inferred instances that

00:23:33.170 --> 00:23:35.660
actually looks at all the
different instances that

00:23:35.660 --> 00:23:38.690
are the same size within a
zone and looks at their usage

00:23:38.690 --> 00:23:44.480
together and tries to create the
idea that these instances that

00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:47.030
were really separate but
running at different times,

00:23:47.030 --> 00:23:50.090
they could have all been run
together as if they were one.

00:23:50.090 --> 00:23:51.567
And it makes no
difference to us.

00:23:51.567 --> 00:23:53.150
So we stack that
together and give you

00:23:53.150 --> 00:23:56.604
the discount for the longest
timespan that we can find.

00:23:56.604 --> 00:23:58.270
The reason we can do
this, incidentally,

00:23:58.270 --> 00:24:01.870
is because for us this
billing, discounts, et cetera,

00:24:01.870 --> 00:24:03.910
is just essentially
a big data problem.

00:24:03.910 --> 00:24:05.200
It runs a big MapReduce.

00:24:05.200 --> 00:24:07.870
And if you remember
what MapReduce is,

00:24:07.870 --> 00:24:11.140
that's what Google calls Hadoop.

00:24:11.140 --> 00:24:14.860
And so it's very
simple for us to just

00:24:14.860 --> 00:24:15.970
have something like this.

00:24:15.970 --> 00:24:18.280
It's complex for us to
process but simple for you

00:24:18.280 --> 00:24:20.890
to take advantage of,
because turns out,

00:24:20.890 --> 00:24:24.200
we like big data
problems at Google.

00:24:24.200 --> 00:24:27.930
So with that said, one of
the things that we saw we

00:24:27.930 --> 00:24:30.079
could make better,
and you'll see--

00:24:30.079 --> 00:24:32.620
I was asked to put the roadmap
note on the top of this slide.

00:24:32.620 --> 00:24:35.570
This is not available quite
yet, but it's coming very soon--

00:24:35.570 --> 00:24:37.100
is we're moving
from a model where

00:24:37.100 --> 00:24:42.660
sustained use discount used
to combine only within a zone.

00:24:42.660 --> 00:24:45.960
We're moving to a model where
it will combine across all

00:24:45.960 --> 00:24:47.880
of the zones within a region.

00:24:47.880 --> 00:24:52.959
And the idea there
is over time, we've

00:24:52.959 --> 00:24:54.750
made it clear that we
believe the right way

00:24:54.750 --> 00:24:57.960
to build a highly
reliable application

00:24:57.960 --> 00:25:01.170
is to spread your workload
across multiple zones.

00:25:01.170 --> 00:25:04.140
And what we've seen is more
and more customers are actually

00:25:04.140 --> 00:25:05.684
doing that.

00:25:05.684 --> 00:25:07.100
But the downside
of that, and this

00:25:07.100 --> 00:25:08.808
goes back to that idea
of fairness again,

00:25:08.808 --> 00:25:11.120
is if you did what
we suggested you

00:25:11.120 --> 00:25:14.570
do to have a great service,
you're going to be giving up

00:25:14.570 --> 00:25:16.220
discount, because
now your machines are

00:25:16.220 --> 00:25:18.080
across different zones.

00:25:18.080 --> 00:25:20.270
And in fact, maybe
you're actually

00:25:20.270 --> 00:25:21.900
not multi-zone for
some of your things,

00:25:21.900 --> 00:25:23.316
but for one reason
or another, you

00:25:23.316 --> 00:25:24.880
decide to move to
a different zone.

00:25:24.880 --> 00:25:29.300
Perhaps, sometimes not all of
our capabilities, like a GPU,

00:25:29.300 --> 00:25:31.700
for example, is available
in every single zone,

00:25:31.700 --> 00:25:33.890
and you've started
adding GPU usage

00:25:33.890 --> 00:25:35.090
and you want to move zones.

00:25:35.090 --> 00:25:37.940
Well, you shouldn't be
penalized because you moved.

00:25:37.940 --> 00:25:40.190
So we're getting
rid of that barrier,

00:25:40.190 --> 00:25:43.700
and we're moving SUD to change
so that it works across all

00:25:43.700 --> 00:25:44.990
the zones in a region.

00:25:44.990 --> 00:25:47.530
And this will be
rolling out pretty soon.

00:25:47.530 --> 00:25:50.030
I'm not going to give an exact
timeline now, but stay tuned.

00:25:50.030 --> 00:25:52.670
There will be a blog
announcement about that.

00:25:52.670 --> 00:25:54.860
And that's just an example
of we found a better way

00:25:54.860 --> 00:25:58.380
and we want to
bring that to you.

00:25:58.380 --> 00:26:01.020
So I talked about those
inferred instances a minute ago.

00:26:01.020 --> 00:26:04.680
And you can imagine that
when we do that rollout,

00:26:04.680 --> 00:26:07.920
these inferences will
now apply across all

00:26:07.920 --> 00:26:09.280
the zones in the region.

00:26:09.280 --> 00:26:12.870
And that's true, except
I'm happy to report

00:26:12.870 --> 00:26:18.202
that I'm going to be able to
retire this slide pretty soon.

00:26:18.202 --> 00:26:19.660
This was one of
the oldest diagrams

00:26:19.660 --> 00:26:20.826
I think we have in our docs.

00:26:20.826 --> 00:26:23.920
It's hard to read.

00:26:23.920 --> 00:26:26.380
Takes a little while to
understand what it means.

00:26:26.380 --> 00:26:28.400
It turns out we have
an even better idea.

00:26:28.400 --> 00:26:30.820
So we're going to be getting
rid of this whole concept

00:26:30.820 --> 00:26:32.680
of inferred instances.

00:26:32.680 --> 00:26:35.290
And what we're doing
instead is something we're

00:26:35.290 --> 00:26:37.980
calling resource-based pricing.

00:26:37.980 --> 00:26:39.860
So we got a lot of
feedback from customers

00:26:39.860 --> 00:26:41.960
that you love Sustained
Use Discounts.

00:26:41.960 --> 00:26:44.600
But that diagram is kind
of hard to understand.

00:26:44.600 --> 00:26:46.850
And more importantly, it's
very hard for you to model.

00:26:46.850 --> 00:26:51.770
So a lot of customers like
to have their own system, be

00:26:51.770 --> 00:26:53.870
it a spreadsheet or
another mechanism,

00:26:53.870 --> 00:26:57.860
that essentially calculates
what our billing system is going

00:26:57.860 --> 00:26:59.750
to calculate and helps
you accurately predict

00:26:59.750 --> 00:27:01.042
what your spend is going to be.

00:27:01.042 --> 00:27:03.333
Maybe that's in order to
model a new application you're

00:27:03.333 --> 00:27:03.860
deploying.

00:27:03.860 --> 00:27:06.292
Maybe it's because you're
considering a migration.

00:27:06.292 --> 00:27:08.750
Or maybe it's just you like to
have a different system that

00:27:08.750 --> 00:27:11.690
keeps track of what you
think your bill should be so

00:27:11.690 --> 00:27:13.550
that you can look
for any anomalies

00:27:13.550 --> 00:27:15.050
and try to understand them.

00:27:15.050 --> 00:27:17.180
So it's very hard to model.

00:27:17.180 --> 00:27:19.160
So we're getting rid of it.

00:27:19.160 --> 00:27:21.079
And what we'll be doing
instead is actually

00:27:21.079 --> 00:27:23.120
a model that's very similar
to what we already do

00:27:23.120 --> 00:27:25.014
with Committed Use Discounts.

00:27:25.014 --> 00:27:27.180
And I'll explain that a
little bit more in a second.

00:27:27.180 --> 00:27:28.910
But essentially,
we're just moving

00:27:28.910 --> 00:27:32.350
to it's all buckets
of vCPUs and RAM.

00:27:32.350 --> 00:27:34.940
And we will look at all
of your usage across time

00:27:34.940 --> 00:27:37.610
and give you the best
Sustained Use Discount we can.

00:27:37.610 --> 00:27:40.010
So the way to think of that
might be low watermarks,

00:27:40.010 --> 00:27:42.620
essentially.

00:27:42.620 --> 00:27:44.530
So I have a simple
scenario of what

00:27:44.530 --> 00:27:46.520
this is going to look like.

00:27:46.520 --> 00:27:48.730
And you can actually see
there's documentation up

00:27:48.730 --> 00:27:49.480
for this today.

00:27:49.480 --> 00:27:52.150
But again, this will take
a little while for us

00:27:52.150 --> 00:27:52.750
to roll out.

00:27:52.750 --> 00:27:54.790
One of the reasons we're
talking about it today

00:27:54.790 --> 00:27:57.769
is because we recognize that
this is a change to the system.

00:27:57.769 --> 00:28:00.310
And even though it's a change
that's completely in customers'

00:28:00.310 --> 00:28:02.680
favor, it's different.

00:28:02.680 --> 00:28:05.382
And so we want to
talk with customers,

00:28:05.382 --> 00:28:07.090
explain what's going
to happen, make sure

00:28:07.090 --> 00:28:09.730
that you're all ready, so
that when this shows up,

00:28:09.730 --> 00:28:11.889
it's not a shock and it's
not a problem if you're

00:28:11.889 --> 00:28:13.680
expecting something
different on your bill.

00:28:13.680 --> 00:28:16.540
So imagine this scenario.

00:28:16.540 --> 00:28:18.160
You're running a
server, you happen

00:28:18.160 --> 00:28:19.630
to use one of our
predefined types.

00:28:19.630 --> 00:28:21.460
Our main line type is
the standard series.

00:28:21.460 --> 00:28:25.180
So you have an n1-standard-4,
it's four vCPUs.

00:28:25.180 --> 00:28:28.390
And this is a database
server for your backend.

00:28:28.390 --> 00:28:30.250
It's not dynamically
autoscaled, but it's

00:28:30.250 --> 00:28:32.740
sized according to what
you expect your load to be.

00:28:32.740 --> 00:28:35.860
But you know that
your CEO is going

00:28:35.860 --> 00:28:37.420
on "Shark Tank" in a week.

00:28:37.420 --> 00:28:40.447
And you're expecting a big
hit in traffic, big jump up.

00:28:40.447 --> 00:28:42.280
And it's going to stay
that way for a while.

00:28:42.280 --> 00:28:44.890
So you'd like to scale
up to a 16 vCPU database

00:28:44.890 --> 00:28:47.680
because you believe that's
what you're going to need.

00:28:47.680 --> 00:28:51.940
So how would Sustained Use
apply in that scenario?

00:28:51.940 --> 00:28:55.412
So in the old model, these two
instance types are different.

00:28:55.412 --> 00:28:57.120
And just assume for
the moment that these

00:28:57.120 --> 00:28:58.410
are the only instances
you're running.

00:28:58.410 --> 00:29:00.640
Obviously, reality's a
little more complicated.

00:29:00.640 --> 00:29:02.280
But let's say that
there weren't really

00:29:02.280 --> 00:29:04.320
any other four vCPU
instances that you

00:29:04.320 --> 00:29:08.950
were running that would infer
together with your database.

00:29:08.950 --> 00:29:11.340
And there weren't any
other 16 vCPU instances

00:29:11.340 --> 00:29:13.902
that you were running before
you upgraded your database.

00:29:13.902 --> 00:29:15.360
Well, in that case,
if you switched

00:29:15.360 --> 00:29:16.890
in the middle of
the month, you'd

00:29:16.890 --> 00:29:20.010
only get the 10% tier
Sustained Use Discounts

00:29:20.010 --> 00:29:23.580
for each of those
machines in that month.

00:29:23.580 --> 00:29:24.569
It's still-- it's nice.

00:29:24.569 --> 00:29:26.610
But if you'd been running
the database all month,

00:29:26.610 --> 00:29:27.610
you would have got 30%.

00:29:27.610 --> 00:29:31.420
So what if we just recognized
that those four cores are still

00:29:31.420 --> 00:29:37.150
four cores, four vCPUs, and give
you the max 30% on those four,

00:29:37.150 --> 00:29:39.700
and then give you that
10% on the other 12?

00:29:39.700 --> 00:29:43.210
And then next month you'd
get 30% on those 16.

00:29:43.210 --> 00:29:45.792
That's essentially
resourced-based pricing.

00:29:45.792 --> 00:29:47.500
And it turns out, like
I said, we already

00:29:47.500 --> 00:29:51.280
count this way if you're
using Committed Use Discounts.

00:29:51.280 --> 00:29:53.980
And in fact, if you are using
Committed Use Discounts,

00:29:53.980 --> 00:29:56.050
then Sustained
Use Discounts also

00:29:56.050 --> 00:29:58.607
already work this
way for any usage

00:29:58.607 --> 00:30:00.190
that goes above your
commitment level,

00:30:00.190 --> 00:30:04.490
because otherwise they
wouldn't play together nicely.

00:30:04.490 --> 00:30:07.460
But now we're just rolling
this out for everybody.

00:30:07.460 --> 00:30:09.850
So all usage will see this.

00:30:12.380 --> 00:30:16.054
So if you'd like to know more,
the docs link is up today.

00:30:16.054 --> 00:30:18.220
There are a couple of things
you should think about,

00:30:18.220 --> 00:30:20.260
how this fits with your
model with your tools.

00:30:20.260 --> 00:30:22.343
I'll give you one example,
which is also mentioned

00:30:22.343 --> 00:30:23.560
in the docs in more detail.

00:30:23.560 --> 00:30:26.200
If you're using
BigQuery billing export,

00:30:26.200 --> 00:30:29.440
if you're looking at the SKU to
understand what kind of machine

00:30:29.440 --> 00:30:32.200
you're using, those SKUs
are going to disappear.

00:30:32.200 --> 00:30:35.560
You won't see an n1-standard-4
SKU usage anymore.

00:30:35.560 --> 00:30:41.140
All it's going to be is
four units of vCPU usage.

00:30:41.140 --> 00:30:45.220
So it will say something like
vCPU North America is one core.

00:30:45.220 --> 00:30:48.160
There is another field,
though, called machine_spec.

00:30:48.160 --> 00:30:51.340
It's a system tag that comes
out that tells you what

00:30:51.340 --> 00:30:52.780
that machine was configured as.

00:30:52.780 --> 00:30:55.300
So if you're looking for
detailed machine information,

00:30:55.300 --> 00:30:57.430
you may want to use that tag--

00:30:57.430 --> 00:31:00.516
sorry, label-- or you may
want to set up your own labels

00:31:00.516 --> 00:31:02.890
to make sure that you have
the metadata you want in order

00:31:02.890 --> 00:31:04.402
to analyze your billing.

00:31:04.402 --> 00:31:06.610
And that's an example of
one of the reasons why we're

00:31:06.610 --> 00:31:10.340
previewing this announcement.

00:31:10.340 --> 00:31:12.110
So we like SUD.

00:31:12.110 --> 00:31:13.940
Our customers like SUD.

00:31:13.940 --> 00:31:18.069
But then they also said,
hey, it resets every month.

00:31:18.069 --> 00:31:20.360
But I know I'm going to be
running this workload for 12

00:31:20.360 --> 00:31:21.560
months or more.

00:31:21.560 --> 00:31:24.980
Can you do you a mega
Sustained Use Discount, just

00:31:24.980 --> 00:31:26.300
super sustained?

00:31:26.300 --> 00:31:28.605
So we thought about that
for a little bit, started

00:31:28.605 --> 00:31:29.480
talking to customers.

00:31:29.480 --> 00:31:31.910
And they said, oh,
yeah, also, can you

00:31:31.910 --> 00:31:34.460
do it without any machine
size restrictions and no zone

00:31:34.460 --> 00:31:35.990
restrictions to
get the discount?

00:31:35.990 --> 00:31:37.760
I want to be able to
move things around.

00:31:37.760 --> 00:31:42.180
And I don't want to pay you
all up front because cash flow.

00:31:42.180 --> 00:31:44.330
So basically, all
the flexibility

00:31:44.330 --> 00:31:45.610
and no restrictions.

00:31:45.610 --> 00:31:47.080
Can we have that?

00:31:47.080 --> 00:31:51.660
So we thought about
that and we said, OK.

00:31:51.660 --> 00:31:54.210
So that's Committed
Use Discounts.

00:31:54.210 --> 00:31:55.754
We introduced this
product last year.

00:31:55.754 --> 00:31:57.420
Again, it was a product
that was in beta

00:31:57.420 --> 00:31:58.890
at the time Next happened.

00:31:58.890 --> 00:32:01.840
And it went GA before
the end of the year.

00:32:01.840 --> 00:32:04.780
Essentially, the concept
here is we give you

00:32:04.780 --> 00:32:07.727
the maximum flexibility and the
maximum discounts that we can.

00:32:07.727 --> 00:32:09.310
All we ask is that
you tell us if it's

00:32:09.310 --> 00:32:10.852
going to be one
year or three years

00:32:10.852 --> 00:32:12.310
that you're willing
to sign up for.

00:32:12.310 --> 00:32:14.620
And the discounts vary
between those two.

00:32:14.620 --> 00:32:15.970
Then you tell us what region--

00:32:15.970 --> 00:32:19.780
not which zone-- but we do have
to do some capacity planning

00:32:19.780 --> 00:32:22.030
to make sure that those
machines are there.

00:32:22.030 --> 00:32:25.600
And then tell us how many
vCPUs and RAM you want.

00:32:25.600 --> 00:32:27.970
And now you'll see
where this is the mirror

00:32:27.970 --> 00:32:31.010
of resource-based pricing
for Sustained Use Discounts.

00:32:31.010 --> 00:32:34.150
It's just vCPUs and RAM.

00:32:34.150 --> 00:32:36.081
You use it however you want.

00:32:36.081 --> 00:32:36.580
That's it.

00:32:39.120 --> 00:32:42.240
So thinking about those
principles I mentioned,

00:32:42.240 --> 00:32:45.019
seems pretty simple, hopefully.

00:32:45.019 --> 00:32:46.560
Just a few decisions
to make that you

00:32:46.560 --> 00:32:48.480
should have a pretty
good idea of if you're

00:32:48.480 --> 00:32:51.302
looking at your workload,
analyzing your bill.

00:32:51.302 --> 00:32:53.760
One of the nice things about
this is because-- what we find

00:32:53.760 --> 00:32:57.510
is that it has enough
flexibility that you can pretty

00:32:57.510 --> 00:33:00.150
safely buy up to
the low watermark

00:33:00.150 --> 00:33:03.300
of your monthly usage, or
usage over some period of time

00:33:03.300 --> 00:33:05.800
that you believe is
representative of your needs,

00:33:05.800 --> 00:33:09.700
and be pretty sure that all
of that is going to be used.

00:33:09.700 --> 00:33:12.720
So we have a number of
customers that tell us

00:33:12.720 --> 00:33:14.190
that they're
regularly seeing well

00:33:14.190 --> 00:33:17.396
above 90% utilization on
their Committed Use Discounts.

00:33:17.396 --> 00:33:19.770
And it's very different than
some other discount products

00:33:19.770 --> 00:33:20.811
that they've played with.

00:33:20.811 --> 00:33:25.820
So the other thing is if
you're growing your deployment,

00:33:25.820 --> 00:33:30.050
you may want to come in
and update that over time

00:33:30.050 --> 00:33:35.180
to cover as much as your
base usage as makes sense.

00:33:35.180 --> 00:33:36.079
So they're flexible.

00:33:36.079 --> 00:33:37.870
You can change shapes
whenever you want to.

00:33:37.870 --> 00:33:40.870
The discount's not
tied to a specific VM.

00:33:40.870 --> 00:33:42.820
You don't have to specify
anything at runtime.

00:33:42.820 --> 00:33:45.241
There's no tagging that says
this VM is a Committed Use

00:33:45.241 --> 00:33:45.740
Discount.

00:33:45.740 --> 00:33:47.110
This one isn't.

00:33:47.110 --> 00:33:51.240
The way it works
is we do it fairly.

00:33:51.240 --> 00:33:54.050
And what fairly
means is we analyze

00:33:54.050 --> 00:33:57.410
this over the entire
time span of your usage.

00:33:57.410 --> 00:34:00.140
And at any point
in time, we take

00:34:00.140 --> 00:34:04.070
the first x vCPUs, and
the first y gigabytes

00:34:04.070 --> 00:34:06.650
of RAM that meet your commitment
and we apply Committed Use

00:34:06.650 --> 00:34:08.190
Discount across all of those.

00:34:08.190 --> 00:34:11.090
If some VM is
temporarily contributing

00:34:11.090 --> 00:34:12.710
to that and that VM
stops and there's

00:34:12.710 --> 00:34:14.929
another one that wasn't
in the consideration,

00:34:14.929 --> 00:34:16.460
it automatically
gets considered.

00:34:16.460 --> 00:34:19.100
So you're always getting
the maximum use of you're

00:34:19.100 --> 00:34:21.080
Committed Use Discounts.

00:34:21.080 --> 00:34:23.270
And then finally, the
Sustained Use Discount

00:34:23.270 --> 00:34:25.429
applies on any usage that's
above that threshold.

00:34:25.429 --> 00:34:28.310
So referring to that idea
I mentioned a minute ago

00:34:28.310 --> 00:34:29.900
of the low watermark,
if you know

00:34:29.900 --> 00:34:32.502
where your baseline is and
you see some usage over that,

00:34:32.502 --> 00:34:34.460
you can rest assured that
that usage will still

00:34:34.460 --> 00:34:36.560
qualify for Sustained
Use Discounts

00:34:36.560 --> 00:34:38.870
if it is a significant
enough period of the month

00:34:38.870 --> 00:34:40.903
to qualify in a normal sense.

00:34:43.611 --> 00:34:45.860
So this was one of the quotes
from our launch article,

00:34:45.860 --> 00:34:48.320
just to give you a sense of
what our customers thought

00:34:48.320 --> 00:34:48.980
about that.

00:34:48.980 --> 00:34:50.380
I think we kind of hit the mark.

00:34:50.380 --> 00:34:51.830
I like the last
sentence the most.

00:34:58.250 --> 00:35:00.289
So another recipe.

00:35:00.289 --> 00:35:02.830
Last year in this talk I would
have said Leverage Preemptible

00:35:02.830 --> 00:35:04.164
VMs.

00:35:04.164 --> 00:35:05.330
This is my favorite product.

00:35:05.330 --> 00:35:06.542
This is our best product.

00:35:06.542 --> 00:35:08.000
This is also the
very first product

00:35:08.000 --> 00:35:11.150
I launched when I got to
Google, so I might be biased.

00:35:11.150 --> 00:35:14.030
But we introduced this
over three years ago.

00:35:14.030 --> 00:35:16.200
And the concept
is really simple.

00:35:16.200 --> 00:35:18.860
If your workload can
tolerate interruptions,

00:35:18.860 --> 00:35:22.610
then we will give you up to
80% off of the cost of the VM

00:35:22.610 --> 00:35:24.710
for you to run a Preemptible VM.

00:35:24.710 --> 00:35:26.110
There's no auctions.

00:35:26.110 --> 00:35:27.280
There's no bidding.

00:35:27.280 --> 00:35:30.310
There's no uncertainty, except
there is a small chance--

00:35:30.310 --> 00:35:33.220
usually around 10%--
that you might get

00:35:33.220 --> 00:35:36.130
preempted with some of your
VMs earlier than the 24 hour

00:35:36.130 --> 00:35:37.430
window.

00:35:37.430 --> 00:35:39.700
So if your workload
is prepared for that,

00:35:39.700 --> 00:35:43.050
this is a great option.

00:35:43.050 --> 00:35:44.800
Now, the reason I'm
not saying VMs anymore

00:35:44.800 --> 00:35:47.637
is because our customers have
told us they love this idea.

00:35:47.637 --> 00:35:49.345
In fact, I was walking
over here earlier,

00:35:49.345 --> 00:35:51.940
and a customer was telling
me, we love Preemptible VMs.

00:35:51.940 --> 00:35:54.160
We can't get enough of them.

00:35:54.160 --> 00:35:55.930
We extended this to
our other resources.

00:35:55.930 --> 00:35:59.860
This is now a general pricing
concept in our infrastructure

00:35:59.860 --> 00:36:01.600
layer of preemptible resources.

00:36:01.600 --> 00:36:04.690
You can also see it reflected
into higher level services that

00:36:04.690 --> 00:36:08.640
use preemptible such as GKE.

00:36:08.640 --> 00:36:13.540
So beyond CPU and
RAM, we now have

00:36:13.540 --> 00:36:16.650
GPUs that you can
get preemptible,

00:36:16.650 --> 00:36:21.800
Cloud TPUs that you can get
preemptible, and local SSD.

00:36:21.800 --> 00:36:24.510
So what those mean is if those
are attached to a Preemptible

00:36:24.510 --> 00:36:27.720
VM, you will get the preemptible
price on those resources.

00:36:27.720 --> 00:36:30.450
And the other rules around the
preemptible shutting down early

00:36:30.450 --> 00:36:31.715
are the same.

00:36:31.715 --> 00:36:32.590
Now, one other thing.

00:36:32.590 --> 00:36:34.423
I had a slide that I
was preparing for this.

00:36:34.423 --> 00:36:36.400
I didn't quite get a
chance to throw it in.

00:36:36.400 --> 00:36:37.900
We made an announcement
today, which

00:36:37.900 --> 00:36:39.274
may have gotten
lost in the noise

00:36:39.274 --> 00:36:42.610
of many other announcements,
that the Cloud TPU prices here

00:36:42.610 --> 00:36:43.476
are actually wrong.

00:36:43.476 --> 00:36:45.850
There's a promotional price
going on right now where that

00:36:45.850 --> 00:36:49.060
normal price is $4.50 and
the PVM price, I believe,

00:36:49.060 --> 00:36:50.500
is down to $1.35.

00:36:50.500 --> 00:36:53.350
So check that out if you're
interested in Cloud TPUs.

00:36:53.350 --> 00:36:56.950
But in general, we
continue extending this.

00:36:56.950 --> 00:36:59.256
And you may see it in
other places in the future.

00:37:02.070 --> 00:37:07.961
So lastly, we'll do a little
Q&amp;A. Thinking about tooling,

00:37:07.961 --> 00:37:09.210
how do you use all this stuff?

00:37:09.210 --> 00:37:11.730
How do you know
that you're getting

00:37:11.730 --> 00:37:14.420
the savings you hope for?

00:37:14.420 --> 00:37:16.340
So the first key-- and
I put this one first,

00:37:16.340 --> 00:37:18.740
not necessarily because it's
the first thing you're going

00:37:18.740 --> 00:37:23.000
to use, but it's the first thing
that I recommend that you do--

00:37:23.000 --> 00:37:25.280
is run to the Cloud
Platform console

00:37:25.280 --> 00:37:27.520
when you start setting
up your project,

00:37:27.520 --> 00:37:29.540
turn on BigQuery Export.

00:37:29.540 --> 00:37:32.009
Now, I will tell you, this
comes with a small cost.

00:37:32.009 --> 00:37:33.800
It is going to store
some data in BigQuery,

00:37:33.800 --> 00:37:35.720
and you have to pay
for that storage.

00:37:35.720 --> 00:37:38.276
But the advantage is if you
turn this on right away,

00:37:38.276 --> 00:37:39.650
then even if you
don't get around

00:37:39.650 --> 00:37:42.920
to starting deep analysis of
your costs for a few months,

00:37:42.920 --> 00:37:45.760
all that data will be there
in BigQuery waiting for you.

00:37:45.760 --> 00:37:47.260
So I'd highly
recommend you do that.

00:37:47.260 --> 00:37:48.922
It gives you much
more information.

00:37:48.922 --> 00:37:51.380
And there's a bunch of tools
that you can use that leverage

00:37:51.380 --> 00:37:54.620
BigQuery that will go
much better for you

00:37:54.620 --> 00:37:57.260
than extremely dense
spreadsheets, which

00:37:57.260 --> 00:37:58.970
is what everybody
seems to use otherwise.

00:37:58.970 --> 00:38:02.060
So I can't underscore
that enough.

00:38:02.060 --> 00:38:07.370
Second of all, I don't
know what this picture is.

00:38:07.370 --> 00:38:10.610
I was thinking maybe it was like
King Tut's tomb or something

00:38:10.610 --> 00:38:12.050
and the different rooms.

00:38:12.050 --> 00:38:15.860
No, actually, this is a
slide I stole from the IO 118

00:38:15.860 --> 00:38:17.810
talk, which I believe
is the one that

00:38:17.810 --> 00:38:19.340
already happened this morning.

00:38:19.340 --> 00:38:23.300
But that talk is all about
how to organize your resources

00:38:23.300 --> 00:38:26.150
using the different features
we have, such as organization

00:38:26.150 --> 00:38:29.060
nodes, projects,
folders, labels,

00:38:29.060 --> 00:38:32.690
so that you can do really
meaningful analysis of how

00:38:32.690 --> 00:38:34.790
your utilization is working.

00:38:34.790 --> 00:38:36.890
So I definitely recommend
checking out that talk.

00:38:36.890 --> 00:38:40.520
And it starts, like I said,
at the two person shop

00:38:40.520 --> 00:38:43.517
and builds up to a large
organization, multiple billing

00:38:43.517 --> 00:38:44.850
accounts, those kinds of things.

00:38:44.850 --> 00:38:47.510
So it's great to
get a sense of, how

00:38:47.510 --> 00:38:51.709
do I organize this so
I can analyze it later?

00:38:51.709 --> 00:38:53.500
Finally, we have some
features around being

00:38:53.500 --> 00:38:55.450
able to set budgets
and then being

00:38:55.450 --> 00:38:58.570
able to get alerts and
notifications on those.

00:38:58.570 --> 00:39:00.870
And then you can have
automated responses as well.

00:39:00.870 --> 00:39:05.515
And so this is a slide that
I stole from the IO103 talk,

00:39:05.515 --> 00:39:06.640
which has not happened yet.

00:39:06.640 --> 00:39:08.170
So I would definitely
recommend checking out

00:39:08.170 --> 00:39:09.214
Stephen's talk as well.

00:39:12.000 --> 00:39:14.490
And there's a rundown of all
the different sessions that

00:39:14.490 --> 00:39:16.198
talk more about all
the different billing

00:39:16.198 --> 00:39:19.530
techniques and tools that you
can use to understand cost.

00:39:19.530 --> 00:39:22.170
I would also recommend--

00:39:22.170 --> 00:39:24.890
which one is it--

00:39:24.890 --> 00:39:29.440
I believe it's IO208 that has
a customer named Vendasta.

00:39:29.440 --> 00:39:31.770
It's Dale and Dale
from Vendasta,

00:39:31.770 --> 00:39:33.140
and they're very funny guys.

00:39:33.140 --> 00:39:37.080
And their talk is,
look at all the--

00:39:37.080 --> 00:39:40.300
as described by them yesterday--
look at all the stuff

00:39:40.300 --> 00:39:41.560
we did wrong.

00:39:41.560 --> 00:39:43.000
Here's how we found it.

00:39:43.000 --> 00:39:44.650
Don't do this.

00:39:44.650 --> 00:39:46.990
So it's pretty interesting
sort of lessons learned

00:39:46.990 --> 00:39:49.444
about analysis and billing.

00:39:49.444 --> 00:39:50.860
Things where they
found, oh, look.

00:39:50.860 --> 00:39:54.520
We were storing 10
terabytes of extra log data

00:39:54.520 --> 00:39:55.690
that we didn't know about.

00:39:55.690 --> 00:39:57.220
And the list goes on.

00:39:57.220 --> 00:40:01.170
